Five requirements, sized before anything is built.

  a support copilot over ten years of tickets
    4,000 requests/day  x  3 fast calls  =  36.0M in, 3.00M out
    cost      $7.20/day   $216/month   $0.0018/request
    index     2,700,000 chunks   16.6 GB of vectors   $21.60 to embed once
    shape     5.4s per request, 0.8 in flight at peak

  a nightly contract-risk enricher
    50,000 requests/day  x  2 fast calls  =  600.0M in, 40.00M out
    cost      $114.00/day   $3,420/month   $0.0023/request
    index     none: each item is read whole
    shape     4.0s per request, 6.9 in flight at peak

  a regulated advisory assistant
    300 requests/day  x  6 smart calls  =  7.2M in, 0.54M out
    cost      $18.72/day   $562/month   $0.0624/request
    index     160,000 chunks   1.0 GB of vectors   $1.28 to embed once
    shape     13.2s per request, 0.1 in flight at peak

  an IT helpdesk agent that can act
    1,500 requests/day  x  5 fast calls  =  37.5M in, 2.25M out
    cost      $6.97/day   $209/month   $0.0046/request
    index     18,000 chunks   0.1 GB of vectors   $0.14 to embed once
    shape     10.0s per request, 0.5 in flight at peak

  meeting summaries inside a video product
    200,000 requests/day  x  2 fast calls  =  4,800.0M in, 240.00M out
    cost      $864.00/day   $25,920/month   $0.0043/request
    index     none: each item is read whole
    shape     16.0s per request, 111.1 in flight at peak

Read what each number decides.

Shape first. 4 of the 5 designs have fewer than ten requests in flight at
their busiest; the largest of those is 6.9. One process with a warm index
would serve any of them, and spend most of its day idle.
The exception is meeting summaries inside a video product, at 111 in flight:
a hundred calls open at once is a rate-limit question, a pooling question and a
failure-domain question before it is a hardware one. A different architecture,
found by arithmetic rather than by an outage.

Then cost. The same fast-model rates give $209 a month for the helpdesk and
$25,920 for the meeting summaries. The regulated assistant pays
13x the input rate for a smart model and still costs $562 a month —
$0.06 a question, which is not the argument in that design.

The enricher's $3,420 a month assumes every contract is re-read every night.
If 1% of contracts are new or changed on a given night, reading only those costs
$34 a month; at a batch discount of half, $17. The envelope asked a
question the requirement did not: does anything need re-reading at all?

And the index separately. The support copilot's 2.7M chunks are 16.6 GB
of vectors before any graph overhead — a storage decision, a startup time, and
the number that decides whether §12.2's approximate index is needed.

Three habits that make an envelope useful rather than decorative:

  size the index separately     it is fixed cost and storage, not throughput
  use Little's law for shape    concurrency is arrival rate times duration,
                                and it selects the deployment target
  put the per-request cost      a monthly total is an argument; a per-request
  beside the monthly one        figure is a design constraint

(The rates above are placeholders. Substitute your provider's and every number
becomes yours — the arithmetic does not change.)
